aving fundamentals as the main evidence", "2028 primary polling shows an active Democratic field but no sign of a statewide Republican breakthrough"], "rationale": "Massachusetts's D+14 partisan lean is the dominant evidence in a race with no polls, no expert ratings, and no fundraising signals, so the forecast stays at the fundamentals-based margin. The open seat removes incumbency effects in both directions, and nothing in recent headlines suggests a Republican competitive position statewide.", "watch": "Any credible statewide general-election polling or expert ratings that show the Republican nominee cutting into the Democratic margin would warrant a shift.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Massachusetts is among the most Democratic-leaning states in the country, and the 2028 presidential contest here is an open-seat race with no incumbent on the ballot. No general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's underlying partisan fundamentals, which point to a Democratic margin of roughly 14 points.\n\nHeadline activity so far centers on the Democratic primary field, with polling showing interest in figures like Pete Buttigieg and Ed Markey, while Republicans appear most enthusiastic about Marco Rubio. Nothing in the current evidence suggests the Republican nominee can close the state's structural gap, though the absence of direct polling keeps uncertainty elevated.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9396, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8954, "margin": 14.0}]}